{"t":1790929846,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"d9911cd8-d1a6-4357-945f-7182f36d2c86","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-10-02T08:02:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 020802\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n402 AM EDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nNo changes to the forecast.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) A stalling cold front may bring rain at times today through\n  Sunday.\n\n- 2) First widespread frost and freeze potential west of the\n  Blue Ridge early next week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A stalling cold front may bring rain at times\ntoday through Sunday.\n\nA cold front will approach from the northwest later this\nafternoon into this evening before eventually pushing south of \nthe area tonight. Showers will likely reach western Maryland by\nthe afternoon, progressing southeastward during the evening and\novernight. Thunderstorms will be few, if any, despite\ntemperatures becoming 15 degrees above average for early\nOctober. A right entrance jet streak later today and a mid-level\nelongated trough could be enough for a few places to encounter\na thunderstorm or two. Otherwise, a westerly component could result\nin downslope drying. Showers could become more scattered the\nfurther east we look later today. Rainfall amounts will be\ngenerally less than a quarter inch for most locations.\n\nStrong high pressure will pass north of the area Saturday while\nthe front stalls across the southeastern states. Southern stream\nmoisture will likely overrun the boundary fairly far to the\nnorth, but there remains some uncertainty in the dividing line\nbetween rain and no rain. Overall, Saturday probably has a\nbetter chance of being dry, especially the further north you go \nwith strong high pressure set up to the north. \n\nA wave of low pressure will likely ride along the boundary \nSaturday night into Sunday, and this may provide a higher chance\nof rain spreading northward into the area. Rain that does occur\nwill likely exit the area Sunday evening.\n\nBeing north of the boundary and potentially having a lot of\ncloud cover, high temperatures will likely remain in the 60s for\nmost areas over the weekend.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... First widespread frost and freeze potential west of \nthe Blue Ridge early next week.\n\nThe true Fall feel sets in next week with highs and lows near to \nbelow normal. Monday will feature broad 1025-1028 mb high pressure \nbuilding east from the Upper Midwest/western Great Lakes behind a \ndeparting upper trof/secondary cold front exiting the Mid-Atlantic \ncoast. The frontal passage itself appears mostly dry with perhaps a \nlingering shower/sprinkle if any moisture is leftover. Breezy west \nto northwest winds will accompany the front with gusts 20-30 mph and \nhigh temperatures in the upper 60s to mid 70s (upper 50s mountains).\n\nHigh pressure moves overhead Monday night into Tuesday allowing the \nwinds to lighten and skies to clear. This will yield a prime \nradiational cooling setup, especially west of the Blue Ridge where \nwidespread frost and freeze conditions are possible. Of particular \nconcern is the high elevations and sheltered valleys (cold sinks) \nwhich could drop down to around freezing. Elsewhere, expect lows in \nthe upper 30s to mid 40s. Locations near the water will be in the \nlow 50s. Similar conditions are expected Tuesday night with \nwidespread mid to upper 30s west of the Blue Ridge and north/west of \nthe I-95 metro areas. These chilly conditions (concern for frost) \nare roughly 1 to 2 weeks earlier than average.\n\nOutside of the frost and freeze potential, dry conditions will \nprevail for much of the week with broad high pressure overhead. A \ncold front looks to cross the region Thursday into Friday, perhaps \nbringing a few showers to the mountains. Low pressure lurking off \nthe Southeast coast bears monitoring, but may stay southeast of the \narea. Otherwise, another large area of high pressure returns for the \nweekend with warming temperatures heading into the following \nworkweek. Highs Tuesday through Wednesday will likely be in the 60s \n(50s mountains), with lows in the 40s to near 50 (30s mountains). \nThursday and Friday, highs warm back into the upper 60s to mid 70s \n(low 60s mountains) with lows in the 40s and 50s.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /07Z FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nVFR conditions are expected through midday today with light \nsoutherly winds. Some marginal LLWS is possible overnight.\n\nA cold front will enter the area later this afternoon or \nevening. Some showers are possible along the front, but they \nwill likely be fairly light. A brief period of MVFR conditions \nis possible tonight.\n\nThe front will stall to the south over the weekend, but its\nposition remains uncertain. Saturday has the greater chance of\nbeing dry, with a wave of low pressure potentially bringing rain\non Sunday. Any sub-VFR conditions would likely be limited to CHO\nSaturday, but could spread northward Sunday.\n\nVFR conditions are expected to prevail Monday through Thursday, \nalthough river valley fog is possible each morning. Winds will be \nout of the north and northwest Monday with gusts around 20 to 25 \nkts. North to northwesterly winds continue Tuesday before shifting \nto the south Wednesday into Thursday. Speeds drop below 15 kts \nduring this time.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nSoutherly winds will uptick a little through today. A Small \nCraft Advisory remains for the waters surrounding southern to \ncentral Maryland through early this morning.\n\nA cold front will push southward tonight, with advisories \npossible in the ensuing northerly flow through parts of \nSaturday. After that, winds may be relatively lighter until a \nsecond cold front pushes through on Monday.\n\nHigh end SCA conditions are possible Monday into Tuesday in the wake \nof a departing cold frontal boundary. Northerly winds will gust 25 \nto 30 kts before dropping off Tuesday morning. A few gusts near gale \nforce are possible if the stronger solutions verify. Sub-SCA \nconditions are expected under high pressure Tuesday afternoon \nthrough Thursday. Winds will switch from the north back to the south \nby the middle of next week.\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\nSome uncertainty continues to exist through today as tide \nlevels remain elevated and subtly fluctuating. These small \nchanges will make the difference in whether there is additional \nminor flooding. Alexandria, Dahlgren, Annapolis, and Havre de \nGrace have the greatest chance of reaching flood stage. The \nevening high tides are favored for any flooding since they are \nastronomically higher. A cold frontal passage should lead to \ndeclining water levels tonight.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...Small Craft Advisory until 10 AM EDT this morning for \n     ANZ532>534-536-537-540>543.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...KLW/DHOF/CJL/EST\nAVIATION...KLW/DHOF/CJL/EST\nMARINE...KLW/DHOF/CJL/EST\n"},"forecast":{"t":1790929799,"days":{"2026-10-02":{"hi":88,"lo":61,"pop":60,"short":"Mostly Sunny then Chance Showers And Thunderstorms"},"2026-10-03":{"hi":68,"lo":59,"pop":36,"short":"Chance Rain Showers then Mostly Cloudy"},"2026-10-04":{"hi":66,"lo":58,"pop":74,"short":"Rain Showers Likely"},"2026-10-05":{"hi":71,"lo":48,"pop":2,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-10-06":{"hi":62,"lo":46,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-07":{"hi":65,"lo":49,"pop":1,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-08":{"hi":71,"lo":null,"pop":1,"short":"Sunny"}}},"grid":{"t0":1790928000,"n":48,"ceil":[null,null,null,null,null,null,24900,null,null,null,24900,24900,11000,11000,6900,9300,8900,7400,7000,6400,6800,8000,8700,9200,9600,9200,8900,8600,8100,8100,8100,7600,7600,7600,8800,8800,8800,11000,11000,11000,14000,14000,14000,18500,18500,18500,10000,10000],"vis":[10.0,10